Does the hypercalcemia with normal differential disfavor chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm (B2) in favor of lymphoid/plasma cell disorders (B3)?", "summary": "Hypercalcemia is more characteristic of plasma cell disorders (B3) than chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms (B2), but the lack of direct evidence for B3 results in moderate_for for B3 and weak_against for B2."}, {"t": 3, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the normal leukocyte differential definitively disfavor plasma cell disorders like multiple myeloma (B3) as the cause of hypercalcemia?", "summary": "Normal differential does not exclude plasma cell disorders (B3) since hypercalcemia in myeloma can occur without peripheral blasts. This finding is neutral for B3."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.435], ["B1", 0.25], ["B2.2", 0.231]]}, {"top": [["B1", 0.388], ["B4", 0.382], ["B3", 0.218]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.349], ["B3", 0.29], ["B4", 0.232]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No immediate life-threatening instability present", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "No branch meets posterior ≥0.75 for confirmation. B1 (posterior 0.388, danger 0.9) and B3 (posterior 0.218, danger 0.85) remain unresolved dangerous alternatives. Tree depth (level 1) is below max_tree_depth (3), and untried discriminators (e.g., bone marrow biopsy, Ph1 chromosome analysis) could further differentiate myeloid neoplasms (B2) vs. lymphoproliferative disorders (B3)."}, "turn_budget_used": 3, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.34904985473905653, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic lymphoma"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chroni